`enclosing` is always the top-level `*dst.FuncDecl`, even when the matched
call sits inside a nested closure/func-literal. A call like
`func A() { go func() { http.Get(url) }() }` reports `enclosing = A`, so
`{{.FuncArgument 0}}` resolves against `A`'s parameters, not the closure's own
(if any). That's a reasonable choice — the closure closes over `A`'s params —
but it's worth spelling out explicitly here so rule authors don't assume
lexically-closest-function semantics.

```suggestion
// walkCallsWithEnclosingFunc visits every *dst.CallExpr in root and invokes fn
// with the call and the top-level *dst.FuncDecl that contains it. Returns nil for
// calls outside any function body, e.g. a package-level variable
// initializer. Note that "enclosing" is always the top-level declaration: a
// call nested inside a closure or func literal is still paired with the
// enclosing top-level function, not the closure itself.
```

