body:
The appended element is `options.Client()`, which is not field-empty: at the pinned
v1.17.9 it returns `&ClientOptions{HTTPClient: httputil.DefaultHTTPClient}`. Since
`MergeClientOptions` merges `HTTPClient` under the same last-non-nil-wins rule, and
this element is deliberately last, its default `HTTPClient` wins over a custom
`http.Client` the caller set on an earlier element (proxy, custom TLS, timeouts,
OIDC/AWS auth transport).

That's the same silent-override class this PR fixes, displaced from `Monitor` onto
`HTTPClient` — and it's newly introduced here: the old per-struct mutation never
constructed a fresh `options.Client()`, so `HTTPClient` was never touched. It also
makes the doc comment above ("can never be overridden by, or silently override,
anything the caller already passed in") inaccurate as written.

A bare `&options.ClientOptions{Monitor: monitor}` alone isn't sufficient either.
v2's `MergeClientOptions` short-circuits on `len(opts) == 1` and returns `opts[0]`
verbatim, so with empty `opts` the injected element becomes the whole merged result
and `HTTPClient` stays nil — and `newClient` does `client.httpClient =
clientOpts.HTTPClient` with no nil fallback. Carrying the effective `HTTPClient`
forward covers that, and preserves pointer identity so the driver's
`c.httpClient == httputil.DefaultHTTPClient` ownership check still behaves.

```suggestion
func injectMonitor(opts []*options.ClientOptions) []*options.ClientOptions {
	merged := options.MergeClientOptions(opts...)
	otelMonitor := otelmongo.NewMonitor()
	monitor := otelMonitor
	if merged.Monitor != nil {
		monitor = chainMonitors(merged.Monitor, otelMonitor)
	}
	// Set only Monitor, and carry the caller's effective HTTPClient forward, so the
	// appended element is a no-op for every field except Monitor. Using
	// options.Client() here would inject its default HTTPClient which, being last in
	// the merge, silently replaces a caller's custom one.
	injected := &options.ClientOptions{Monitor: monitor, HTTPClient: merged.HTTPClient}
	// Full slice expression forces a new backing array so this never mutates a
	// caller-owned slice passed in via `opts...`.
	return append(opts[:len(opts):len(opts)], injected)
}
```

Worth a regression test alongside the existing ones: caller sets a custom
`HTTPClient` on an earlier element, assert `MergeClientOptions(newOpts...).HTTPClient`
is still that client. No current test asserts any non-`Monitor` field survives
injection, which is why this slipped through.

